]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Btfs
is a Bluetooth Low Energy stack.  All of it above the controller is
an ordinary user program: the kernel carries HCI packets and does
nothing else, which it already knows how to do \- a byte stream
through
.IR devuart (3)
for a UART-attached controller, endpoints through
.IR devusb (3)
for a USB one.
.PP
Bluetooth's own structure is a hierarchy \- a controller, the devices
it can hear, the services each device has, the characteristics inside
a service \- so
.I btfs
serves it as one, and that name space is the whole interface.  There
are no other calls: a program uses Bluetooth here by opening, reading
and writing files.
.PP
.I Controller
names the transport.  For the default H4 transport it is a byte
stream and, optionally, its speed and whether to use RTS/CTS flow
control:
.IP
.EX
bt/btfs /dev/eia1!115200!fc
.EE
.PP
It may instead be a
.IR dial (2)
string, which is how a machine whose only serial port is its console
reaches a controller elsewhere; the framing is the same either way.
.PP
Several may be named, and each becomes one numbered directory:
.IP
.EX
bt/btfs /dev/eia1!115200!fc /dev/eia2!115200!fc
.EE
.PP
gives
.B /net/bt/0
and
.BR /net/bt/1 .
They are two radios in one program and not two programs, so the node
table, the mount point and the key file are shared while the devices,
the links, the scanning and the bonds are not.
With
.B -u
the transport is USB instead, and
.I controller
is the device directory under
.B /dev/usb
.RB ( ep3.0 ,
say), or empty to take the first Bluetooth controller found there.
.PP
.B -5
selects the H5 transport, the three-wire one, instead of H4.  H4 puts
a type byte in front of a packet and trusts the wire; H5 adds SLIP
framing, a header with sequence and acknowledgement numbers, and
retransmission, which is what a module speaks when it has no hardware
flow control to spare.  Nothing above the transport changes.
.B -m
gives the mount point, default
.BR /net/bt .
.B -k
names a file the bonding keys are read from at start and written back
to as they are made; without it they last only as long as
.I btfs
does.
.SS The name space
.TF /net/bt/0/remote/ADDR/gatt
.PD
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/addr
The controller's own address.  There is one numbered directory per
controller, in the order the devices were given on the command line,
and nothing is shared between them: a device heard on one radio, a
link, a scan and a bond all belong to the radio they happened on.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/version
What the controller says it is.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/state
One line: the address, whether it is scanning, whether an inquiry is
running, whether it is advertising, how many devices have been heard,
how many connections are open, and the controller's ACL packet size
and buffer count.  A
.B scodropped
count is added when the controller has sent any SCO packets: there is
no audio here, nothing above the transport has a use for them, and
they are counted rather than dropped in silence, where they could not
be told from a transport losing them.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/scan
.B on
or
.BR off .
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/bonds
One line per bond: the peer, its address type, the keys, and
.BI on " addr" ,
the controller the bond was made on.  A long term key is agreed
between one peripheral and one radio, and the peripheral stores this
side's identity address alongside it, so a bond made on one radio is
not a bond on another and is not offered to it.
.IP
There are two places the bonds can be kept, and
.B -k
and
.B -K
are exclusive.  The key file named by
.B -k
is written by
.I btfs
itself and holds every controller's bonds together.
.B -K
instead names a mounted
.IR btkeyfs (4):
.I btfs
then holds no key file at all, reads the bonds from the service at
startup and sends each new one to it as it is made.  That is the
better of the two \- the store outlives this
.IR btfs ,
two of them on two sets of radios share it, and the keys are not
readable by everything that can read a radio \- and the file is kept
because a machine with no key service mounted still has to be able to
pair.  Both hold the same text.
.IP
Either way each line says which controller its bond was made on; a
line that does not say, from a store written when there was only one
controller to make a bond on, is offered to every controller and
.I btfs
says so once.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/ctl
Writing
.B "scan on"
or
.B "scan off"
starts and stops scanning, which is the LE half of finding devices.
.B "inquiry on"
is the Classic half, and is not a switch: a scan runs until it is
stopped and hears whatever advertises, while an inquiry runs for a
length the controller is told \- about ten seconds \- and then says it
is over, which is when
.B state
says
.B "inquiry off"
again.
.B "inquiry off"
cancels one early.  What answers appears under
.B remote
like anything else, with
.B type
saying
.BR classic .
A device that sent an extended inquiry response has its name already;
one that did not is asked, over a page that does not become a
connection, so its name turns up a moment after it does.
.B connect
on such a device's own
.B ctl
opens a BR/EDR link to it and
.B disconnect
takes it down, and a peer that pages this side is accepted the same
way an LE peer that connects is.
.B state
says
.B br/edr
where an LE link would give its connection parameters, because it has
none.  What runs over such a link is L2CAP and nothing above it: ATT
is LE only, so there is no
.B gatt
tree on a Classic device.
.B "connect \fIaddr\fP [public|random]",
.BI "disconnect " addr
and
.BI "pair " addr
act on one device.
.B pair
means two different things on the two kinds of link.  On LE it is SMP,
run here, over the link.  On BR/EDR the controller runs it and asks
this side questions on the way \- have you a key for this device, what
can you display and type, do you agree to this number \- and the
answers come from the bonds, from
.B -k
or
.BR -K .
Either way it is Just Works: there is nothing here that can show a
number or read one, so the only honest answer to the confirmation is
yes, and what that gives is an encrypted link with no protection
against a man in the middle.  A BR/EDR link is encrypted after it is
authenticated, because a link where the key was agreed and never used
is not what any profile means by a secure link.
.IP
The old kind of Classic pairing, where a controller asks for a PIN, is
refused and said out loud.  A device that will do nothing else cannot
be paired with here.
.BI "params " "min max latency timeout"
on a device's own
.B ctl
asks the controller to run the link at those parameters: the
connection interval in units of 1.25ms, how many connection events the
peripheral may skip, and the supervision timeout in units of 10ms.
Values a controller would refuse are refused here instead of being
sent.  A peripheral cannot change them itself and asks over L2CAP; a
request that is accepted is carried out, which is why one that cannot
be is refused rather than accepted and forgotten.
.BI "service " uuid
adds a primary service to the database this side serves, and
.BI "characteristic " "service uuid props"
adds a characteristic to it \-
.I props
being the letters
.BR r ,
.B w
(write with a response),
.B W
(write without one),
.B n
and
.BR i .
The service is named again on each characteristic rather than being
remembered between writes, so two programs writing
.B ctl
cannot put a characteristic in each other's service.  Handles are
given out as attributes are added and never given out again, so what a
connected peer already holds stays right; what it has not seen it is
told about, by the Service Changed indication every database carries.
The database may therefore grow while a peer is connected, and a peer
that subscribed walks it again.
.B "advertise on"
and
.B "advertise off"
start and stop advertising, and
.BI "advname " name
sets the name the advertisement carries; it can be changed while
advertising is running and the peer sees the new one on the next
advertisement.  Advertising is connectable: a peer that finds this
side can open a link to it, and that link appears under
.B remote
like any other, with
.B state
saying
.B peripheral
because the peer opened it rather than this side.  .BI "l2cap " psm
on a device's own
.B ctl
opens an LE credit-based channel to that PSM, written as four
hexadecimal digits, and
.BI "unl2cap " psm
closes it.  A channel that the peer refuses fails with the peer's own
reason \(em
.B "PSM not supported"
and the rest \(em rather than with a word of this program's choosing.
.B connect
waits for the controller to establish the link and fails with
.B "connection timed out"
followed by what the controller said, if it said anything; a
connection that completes just as the wait runs out is returned rather
than thrown away, and one that completes after that is closed when it
is found.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/
A device that has been heard from, named by its address written most
significant byte first \- or, once a bond gives it one, by its
identity address, so a device that rotates a resolvable private
address keeps one directory rather than gaining one every few minutes.
It holds
.BR addr ,
which is the address it is answering on now and changes under a
rotating device,
.B identity
(the identity address behind it and the address it was resolved from,
or
.B none
for a device that has not distributed an identity key),
.B type
(public or random, or
.B classic
followed by
.BI class " hhhhhh"
for a device heard on an inquiry, whose address is a BD_ADDR and has
no LE type, and whose class of device is the only thing an inquiry
says about what it is),
.B rssi
and
.B name
from its advertisement,
.B adv
(the whole advertisement in hexadecimal),
.BR state ,
and a
.B ctl
that takes
.BR connect ,
.B disconnect
and
.B pair
without an address.
.B state
says how long ago the device was last heard from when it is not
connected, and what the link is running at when it is, and which side
opened it \-
.B central
for a link this side opened,
.B peripheral
for one a peer opened to it.
.PP
A device that stops answering is forgotten after the time given by
.B -a
(five minutes by default; 0 keeps everything), so a scan in a busy
room does not fill this directory for ever.  Three things are never
forgotten: a connected device, because it is plainly there and an
advertisement is not how it is being talked to; a device with an
identity from a bond, because a bond exists in order to find that
device again; and every device while scanning is off, because then
nothing is arriving and age says nothing about what is there.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/gatt/
Appears when an LE device is connected: a directory per service,
named by its UUID, holding a file per characteristic, named by its
UUID.  A Classic device has no
.B gatt
tree \- ATT is LE only \- and has
.B sdp
instead, described below.
A service may hold the same UUID twice \- HID over GATT gives every
report the same one \- and then every characteristic with that UUID is
named
.BI uuid . hhhh
instead, where
.I hhhh
is its value handle in four hexadecimal digits; a UUID that occurs
once keeps its bare name.  A characteristic's descriptors, other than
the client configuration, are files beside it named
.IB name . uuid \fR;
the client configuration is not one of them, since it is not a value
to read but the subscription, and the
.B .notify
file is how that is offered.
If the device has a Service Changed characteristic it is subscribed
to, and an indication on it rebuilds this whole directory on the same
link \- so a device that rearranges its database is followed rather
than believed on the copy walked first.  Files open across that are
gone, including
.B .notify
streams, whose subscriptions stood against handles that may no longer
mean the same thing; a client that wants one reopens it.  The
subscription to Service Changed itself is not rewritten unless its own
descriptor moved, since it belongs to the link rather than to a walk
of the database.
A service that includes others has an
.B included
file listing them, one per line as UUID, first handle and last, and
each is a directory here too \- including a secondary service, which
primary discovery does not report and which would otherwise be
unreachable.
.PP
A value longer than the link's ATT MTU is read in pieces with Read
Blob and written in pieces with Prepare Write, committed with one
Execute Write, so neither direction is limited by the MTU.
Reading one reads the characteristic off the device; writing one
writes it.
.PD
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/gatt/
The database this side serves, which is
.B remote/\fIADDR\fP/gatt
read the other way round: a directory per service, named by its UUID,
holding a file per characteristic, named by its UUID.  Reading one
reads what a peer reading that characteristic would get; writing one
sets what a peer will read next.  These files are always writable
whatever the characteristic's properties say \- the properties are
what a
.I peer
may do, and this is how the owner of the database sets the value at
all.
.PP
A peer that connects to this side reads and writes them.  Discovery,
reads, reads of values longer than the link's MTU, and writes are
answered; anything else is answered with
.B "Request Not Supported" ,
which is what a server says and what a client can act on.  A write
from a peer to a characteristic whose properties do not allow one is
refused with
.BR "Write Not Permitted" .
.PP
A characteristic whose properties include
.B n
or
.B i
carries the descriptor a peer writes to ask for notifications, and
writing the file here is what sends them: every peer that asked gets
one.  A peer that asked for indications is waited for, since only one
indication may be outstanding at a time.  The subscription belongs to
the link and not to the database, so two peers reading the same
characteristic may differ on whether they want to be told about it.
.PP
Every database carries a Generic Attribute service holding Service
Changed, at handles one to four, so a peer has something to subscribe
to before anything else exists.  It is not shown here \- it is not
part of what is being published, it is how a change to what is
published is announced.
.PP
A peer that connects may also pair.  This side answers as the
responder, hands the controller the key when the peer starts
encryption, and distributes a long term key; what comes of it is a
bond like any other, in
.B bonds
and in the store, naming the peer and the controller it was made
on.  It is Just Works, so it gives no protection against a man in the
middle, exactly as pairing in the other direction does not.
.PP
A peer that has paired before does not pair again: it starts
encryption naming the key it was given, and that key is found in the
bond and handed to the controller.  A key that is named and not held
is answered with the negative reply, which is what tells the peer to
pair afresh rather than leaving it waiting.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/sdp/
Appears when a Classic device is connected and answered a service
discovery search: one file per service record, named by the record's
handle in eight hexadecimal digits.  It is what a Classic device has
in place of a
.B gatt
tree, and it is not the same kind of thing: a GATT database is walked
and can be walked again, while SDP is asked once and answers with
whole records, so these files are what the device said at the moment
the link came up.
.PP
Each file holds the record as lines.
.BI class " uuid"
for each service class it claims, with a name after the UUID when
there is one to give;
.BI name " text"
if it named itself;
.BI proto " name parameters"
for each protocol it is reached over, lowest first \- which is where
an RFCOMM channel number comes from and the reason to read a record at
all; and then
.BI attr " id value"
for every attribute the device gave, whether or not anything here
knows what it means, because a record holds whatever the device put in
it.
.PP
The search is for the public browse group, which is where a service
that wants to be found puts itself.  A device with services outside it
has them and does not advertise them, and a device with nothing in it
has no
.B sdp
directory rather than an empty one.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/rfcomm/\fIN\fP/data
A serial port on server channel
.IR N ,
opened by writing
.BI "rfcomm " N
to the device's own
.B ctl
and closed with
.BI "unrfcomm " N .
The channel number is what an
.B sdp
record's
.B proto
line gives, so a device is found with service discovery and reached
with this.
.PP
It is a stream and not a record: what is written is bytes and what is
read is bytes, and a write longer than the frame size the peer agreed
to is cut into frames here, so a read may return less than the write
that produced it.  A write may wait, because RFCOMM has its own
credits underneath, negotiated rather than granted.  The multiplexer
under all this \- an L2CAP channel to PSM 3 and a session on it \- is
started by the first port and shared by the rest: thirty ports on one
channel is what a session is.
.TP
.B /net/bt/\fIN\fP/remote/\fIADDR\fP/l2cap/
One file per open LE credit-based channel, named by its PSM in four
hexadecimal digits.  This is not part of the GATT tree and does not go
away when the device says its database has moved: a channel is not an
attribute and a Service Changed indication says nothing about it.  It
does go away with the link, along with everything else the link was
carrying.
.PP
The channel on a BR/EDR link is a basic mode one and the file is the
same file: one write is one SDU, one read is the next SDU whole, and
the difference is underneath.  A basic mode channel is opened with a
connection request and then configured in both directions, and is not
open until both have been answered; it has no credits, so a write
that fits goes out at once, and the size it must fit in is the MTU the
peer asked for in its configuration.  Nothing here serves a PSM, so a
peer that asks for a channel on a link this side is on is answered
.B "PSM not supported" .
.PP
One write is one SDU and one read is the next SDU whole.  A write may
wait: credits are how the peer says it is ready, and a peer that has
granted none has said it is not, so the bytes wait rather than being
thrown away.  A read that offers a smaller buffer than the SDU fails
saying how big the SDU was, because there is nowhere to put the
remainder and a short read would lose it silently; the most this side
will accept is 512 bytes, so a reader that offers that much never sees
it.  The device's
.B state
has a line per open channel giving the PSM, both channel identifiers,
and the SDU and PDU sizes the two sides settled on.
.PP
The flow control is real in both directions.  Going out, an SDU is cut
into PDUs at the peer's maximum and a credit is spent on each.  Coming
in, credits are granted for as many PDUs as there is room for and
given back when a reader takes the SDU they carried, so nothing is
ever dropped for want of somewhere to put it \(em unlike the fixed
channels above, where a consumer that falls behind loses frames
because the alternative is stopping the controller.  A peer that sends
a segment it has no credit for, or announces an SDU longer than the
MTU, has broken the only rule that bounds this side's memory, and the
channel is closed with that reason rather than the segment absorbed.
.PP
So an echo service on PSM 0x0080 is
.IP
.EX
echo l2cap 0080 > /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/ctl
echo hello > /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/l2cap/0080
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/l2cap/0080
.EE
.PP
A characteristic that notifies or indicates also has a
.IB uuid .notify
beside it.  Opening that file subscribes to the characteristic, each
read returns the next notification whole, and closing it
unsubscribes.
.PP
So a battery level is
.IP
.EX
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/gatt/180f/2a19
.EE
.PP
and watching it change is
.IP
.EX
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/gatt/180f/2a19.notify
.EE
.SS Bthid
.I Bthid
turns a Bluetooth keyboard or mouse into keystrokes on
.B /dev/keyboard
and motion on
.BR /dev/pointer .
It takes the first connected device with a
.B 1812
service, or the device directory named on its command line, and looks
for the boot keyboard and boot mouse input reports.  If either is
there it writes 0 to the Protocol Mode characteristic to ask for the
boot protocol and reads the reports off their
.B .notify
files.  The reports are what is looked at first and the write only
follows finding one: whether a device has a boot protocol is a
question about its boot reports, and writing 0 to a device that has
none would put it in a mode it cannot report in.
.PP
There is no Bluetooth in it: the device is a directory, a
subscription is an open, and a report is a read.  The translation of
reports to keys and motion is
.IR hidboot (2)
and
.IR hidreport (2),
both shared with
.IR usbd (1),
because above the transport the reports are identical.
.PP
A boot report is the state of the keyboard and not an event \- the
keys that are down, in no order \- so a key that is held appears in
every report until it is let go, and only keys that were not down
before are typed.
.PP
A device with no Protocol Mode characteristic, or with no boot report
behind it, speaks only the report protocol, and then the layout is
read off the device.  The
.B 2a4b
Report Map characteristic is a HID report descriptor; each
.B 2a4d
Report characteristic has a
.B 2908
Report Reference descriptor beside it saying which report id it
carries and whether it is an input.  Putting the two together is what
says which report is the keyboard and which the pointer, and where in
each report the modifiers, the key codes, the buttons and the axes
are.  It is not an optimisation: every Report characteristic has the
same UUID, so nothing else distinguishes them, and a device is free to
give the mouse the lower id.  A report that is neither a keyboard nor
a pointer is left alone.
.PP
.B -k
and
.B -p
name files to write to instead of the real devices, which is what the
bench uses;
.B -v
says what each report became.
.SS Btbench
.I Btbench
starts
.I btfs
on the transport it is given and then drives the whole stack through
its files, printing what happens at each step: the controller, a
scan, what answered, a connection, the remote device's whole GATT
database, a characteristic read, three notifications, pairing, and a
disconnection.  It is the test program, and it runs unchanged under
.IR emu (1)
and inside the native kernels.
